1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,920
The history of auto racing is littered with spectacular and deadly crashes.

2
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:19,800
But today, a high-tech array of safety gear has evolved to separate victory from disaster.

3
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:28,840
Now, racetrack tech on Modern Marvels.

4
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,400
In auto racing, highly evolved breeds of automobiles push the limits of mechanical engineering

5
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,100
and aerodynamics.

6
00:01:09,101 --> 00:01:13,880
Equipped with the latest technology, race teams fine tune each machine in search of

7
00:01:13,881 --> 00:01:15,620
a winning edge.

8
00:01:15,621 --> 00:01:19,840
Which in racing means one thing, speed.

9
00:01:19,841 --> 00:01:24,520
Unfortunately, there's no guarantee of making it to the finish line.

10
00:01:24,521 --> 00:01:29,840
The sport's risks are as clearly defined as the black and white on a checkered flag.

11
00:01:29,841 --> 00:01:33,400
racing professionals understand the dangers they face.

12
00:01:33,401 --> 00:01:38,960
They also know that hidden in the twisted mass of carbon fiber and steel lie clues that

13
00:01:38,961 --> 00:01:44,220
can lead the way to designing safer race cars.

14
00:01:44,221 --> 00:01:49,440
That's why today there's a new kind of crash science developing in motorsports.

15
00:01:49,441 --> 00:01:54,640
It's a track savvy brand of detective work that allows crash scene investigators to draw

16
00:01:54,641 --> 00:01:58,800
valuable safety lessons from every pile of wreckage.

17
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:03,300
There's all kinds of things they can go back now and look at, hard evidence, break it down,

18
00:02:03,301 --> 00:02:09,900
analyze it, almost make it look almost as if it's revisiting the scene of a crime.

19
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:15,200
From the crash scene, it's off to the laboratory, where researchers conceive and test the latest

20
00:02:15,201 --> 00:02:17,200
safety innovations.

21
00:02:17,201 --> 00:02:22,180
They blend data gathered at the track with space-age materials to create newer, better

22
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:23,820
safety gear.

23
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:29,220
This gear is constantly evolving because for every technological breakthrough, there's

24
00:02:29,221 --> 00:02:32,920
another breakdown waiting just around the next turn.

25
00:02:32,921 --> 00:02:34,760
There's an action and a reaction.

26
00:02:34,761 --> 00:02:38,780
What you think is the best today may not be the best tomorrow.

27
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:45,140
Auto racing began in Europe in the mid-1890s with cars running neck and neck at a top speed

28
00:02:45,141 --> 00:02:48,380
of 11 miles per hour.

29
00:02:48,381 --> 00:02:53,120
Around the turn of the century, American and European racers began competing on tracks

30
00:02:53,121 --> 00:02:54,900
like the Brickyard in Indianapolis.

31
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:59,040
These new tracks became showplaces for the best cars

32
00:02:59,041 --> 00:03:01,020
and the biggest egos.

33
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:06,500
Well, in the early days, there was a whole lot of men were men,

34
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:10,060
and that was the whole philosophy behind racing.

35
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:11,880
I mean, it was who could be the biggest man

36
00:03:11,881 --> 00:03:15,380
and had the biggest ego to get out there

37
00:03:15,381 --> 00:03:17,040
and really go after it to go racing.

38
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:19,920
The drivers had to be fearless

39
00:03:19,921 --> 00:03:23,160
because even at speeds of just 50 miles per hour,

40
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:26,040
cars were prone to devastating equipment failure.

41
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:29,560
One of the most common parts to fail were the tires.

42
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:32,880
So the quest to make a better race tire

43
00:03:32,881 --> 00:03:36,000
became one of the first safety challenges in motorsports.

44
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:41,060
Engineers at one of the early tire manufacturers, Goodyear,

45
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:44,040
determined that tire failure was caused by a deficiency

46
00:03:44,041 --> 00:03:45,980
in the tire's square woven fabric.

47
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:48,380
As a race car rounded a corner,

48
00:03:48,381 --> 00:03:51,360
the force of the turn would cause the fabric to tear.

49
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,860
By switching to a cord fabric design,

50
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:58,520
Goodyear found that the tires could flex in the turns without tearing.

51
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,940
But this innovation, like so many in early racing,

52
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:06,000
was soon made obsolete by the sport's increasing speeds.

53
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:11,100
From dirt ovals in the south to the Grand Prix courses in Europe,

54
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:15,340
race teams were obsessed only with building faster cars.

55
00:04:15,341 --> 00:04:23,440
And while the technology of speed zoomed into the future, the development of safety gear remained mired in the past.

56
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:29,580
In the 1940s, drivers wore flimsy helmets with liners made of pressed cork.

57
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:36,960
And up until the 1960s, racers buckled up with a single lap belt and dressed in flammable cotton suits.

58
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:44,440
Finally, in the 1970s, racing began to fully embrace new innovations in safety.

59
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:51,840
Drivers started wearing modern protective fire suits, stronger helmets, and shoulder harness seat belts.

60
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:58,840
The new safety gear was often custom made by the race teams and drivers themselves.

61
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:07,200
It was a bunch of guys that saw things and the patterns that occurred with a series or sequence of crashes.

62
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:11,540
And it was friends of friends of friends that said, hey, look, we need to do something here.

63
00:05:11,541 --> 00:05:18,100
It would not be until the late 1980s that the study of racing safety became a recognized science.

64
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:22,920
By then, track speeds were topping 200 miles per hour.

65
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,820
And with the increased speed came increased dangers.

66
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:30,620
It was clear that something had to be done about safety.

67
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:35,860
So race officials teamed up with the biomechanical engineers working in the auto industry.

68
00:05:35,861 --> 00:05:46,531
We had the sleds, we had the dummies, and we had the recording devices that allowed us to put black box recorders into the race cars and begin to collect what turns

69
00:05:46,532 --> 00:05:50,500
out to be super valuable data on real people in real crashes.

70
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:59,140
Crash investigators began using high-speed cameras to slow things down and see the method within the madness of a dramatic crash.

71
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:04,260
Through their research, they defined two major categories of crashes.

72
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:08,760
In a dispersed crash, the car crumples into pieces that fly off.

73
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:14,180
As this happens, the force of the impact is transferred away from the driver.

74
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:19,340
In a focused crash, the force of the impact is channeled in a single direction.

75
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:26,480
Driver Alex Zanardi experienced this type of crash in 2001 at Germany's Eurospeedway.

76
00:06:26,481 --> 00:06:37,900
I think the thing with the Zanardi accident was it was really just, you know, two cars at a certain angle at the wrong place at the wrong time.

77
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:43,920
As Zanardi pulled out of the pits, he was struck by an oncoming racer at full speed.

78
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:46,880
The impact tore off the front end of his car.

79
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:50,260
Zanardi lost both of his legs in the accident.

80
00:06:50,261 --> 00:06:57,260
After carefully studying the crash, officials decided to add even more carbon fiber reinforcement

81
00:06:57,261 --> 00:07:03,260
to the sidewall of the racing tub that surrounds the driver in Indy-style cars.

82
00:07:03,261 --> 00:07:10,260
The extra carbon fiber became known as the Zanardi bar, and it reinforced the midsection of the car.

83
00:07:10,261 --> 00:07:19,260
In 2002, an accident at the Australian race in Surfer's Paradise demonstrated the Zanardi bar's effectiveness in a crash.

84
00:07:19,261 --> 00:07:24,060
But the dramatic collision also showed that even more protection was needed.

85
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,980
At Surfer's Paradise, the track was wet and visibility was limited.

86
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:36,540
By the time Japanese driver Tora Takagi came upon a multi-car accident, there was no time to react.

87
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:40,520
And as you can see, this is the first part of the impact.

88
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:42,700
You can just see the wheel.

89
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:45,820
There's the shape of the wheel and there's the axle.

90
00:07:45,821 --> 00:07:52,240
In the second phase of the crash, the tub of Takagi's car was T-boned by another oncoming racer.

91
00:07:52,241 --> 00:07:58,620
The force of the impact penetrated Takagi's carbon tub and flipped his car upside down.

92
00:07:58,621 --> 00:08:06,460
In the aftermath of the crash, Takagi's race team was able to examine the point of penetration just beneath the Zanardi bar.

93
00:08:06,461 --> 00:08:10,480
And here we've got a safety system being added.

94
00:08:10,481 --> 00:08:15,820
and guess what? This nose is actually gone in underneath the patch.

95
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:19,500
It's gone below the area. So what does that need to do?

96
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:26,100
That needs to carry on around here to add some safety to the below that patch

97
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:29,340
because not all of the accidents go in here or go in here.

98
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:33,780
They can go anywhere. So you might say it looks obvious now,

99
00:08:33,781 --> 00:08:39,360
but this is the first time anybody's ever seen this kind of destruction.

100
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:47,040
Takagi's car was destroyed, but the Zanardi bar helped it hold together well enough to prevent any serious injuries.

101
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,800
Takagi was back racing the following week.

102
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:57,660
And today, car designers are still analyzing how to prevent future penetrations of the carbon fiber tub.

103
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:04,760
Car-to-car crashes aren't the only ones that leave safety engineers scratching their heads.

104
00:09:04,761 --> 00:09:13,880
In fact, some of the most perplexing and deadly crashes occur when cars slam into the walls that line the track.

105
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:17,760
In the early days, some tracks had no walls.

106
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:24,660
But after cars started crashing into the spectators, barriers were added to protect the race fans.

107
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:32,120
Wooden, straw, and plain old dirt barriers helped absorb the brunt of the impact,

108
00:09:32,121 --> 00:09:35,020
but didn't always stop a tumbling car.

109
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:39,500
So tracks began building the concrete barriers that are still in use today.

110
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,820
Concrete walls can stop a car dead in its tracks,

111
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:48,180
but the blunt force of the impact causes serious injuries to drivers.

112
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:59,220
At the University of Nebraska, engineers are testing a new type of track wall.

113
00:09:59,380 --> 00:10:01,460
It's called the Safer Barrier.

114
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:06,120
SAFER stands for Steel and Foam Energy Reduction.

115
00:10:06,121 --> 00:10:12,120
The barrier is constructed of rectangular steel tubes welded together to form a unified element.

116
00:10:12,121 --> 00:10:22,120
Bundles of two-inch thick sheets of extruded closed cell polystyrene are placed in modules between the steel tubing and the existing concrete wall.

117
00:10:22,121 --> 00:10:31,120
We use these strategically spaced behind the steel skin to absorb the impact event that's coming in with the crash vehicle.

118
00:10:31,121 --> 00:10:36,480
We're trying to make it less strenuous on the drivers in the race cars

119
00:10:36,481 --> 00:10:40,160
and trying to reduce the potential for serious injuries.

120
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:44,600
Dr. Dean Sicking's team at the University of Nebraska

121
00:10:44,601 --> 00:10:50,260
uses test dummies and real Indy race cars to study the safer barriers' effectiveness.

122
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,200
Each car is outfitted with crash recorders,

123
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:56,820
which are linked to sensors installed throughout the car.

124
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:02,420
These sensors monitor the force loads as the energy of the crash rips through the car.

125
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:10,860
The force of the impact is also measured with accelerometers embedded inside the head of the crash test dummy.

126
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:16,700
I once described this dummy as the fastest riding dummy on Earth.

127
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:22,800
In the head, there are three accelerometers mounted at the CG, or center gravity of the skull,

128
00:11:22,801 --> 00:11:27,540
measuring accelerations in three directions, the frontal, side to side, up and down.

129
00:11:28,140 --> 00:11:34,160
The data gathered by the crash recorders is assembled in a way that lets researchers know how well the barriers are working.

130
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:36,000
Now, Dr.

131
00:11:36,001 --> 00:11:42,500
Sikin is preparing to crash real Indy race cars into both a concrete wall and a new, safer barrier.

132
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:48,200
He will then compare data and see where improvements to the barrier system can be made.

133
00:11:48,201 --> 00:11:55,600
We're going to try to crash this into a radius concrete barrier at 150 miles per hour and 25 degrees.

134
00:11:55,601 --> 00:12:02,080
The cameras are in place, and the car hurls toward the wall at 150 miles per hour.

135
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:07,900
The devastation is quick and complete.

136
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,900
Next, Dr. Sicking's research team sends an identical car into the safer barrier.

137
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:18,140
The second crash appears just as destructive as the first.

138
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:23,200
But computer readings from the car's crash recorders tell a different story.

139
00:12:23,201 --> 00:12:29,200
At the point of impact, the safer barrier begins to absorb the force of the crash.

140
00:12:29,201 --> 00:12:34,200
This force dissipates as the crash continues and protects the driver in the cockpit.

141
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:47,200
Similar tests using stock cars offer the same promising results.

142
00:12:48,200 --> 00:13:00,020
The vast majority of injuries and fatalities over the last 10 years have involved the outside barriers on the outsides of the turns.

143
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:01,740
And so that's been our focus.

144
00:13:01,741 --> 00:13:10,920
As we move forward and we tackle this problem and we achieve success in that we dramatically reduce injury and fatalities for those impacts,

145
00:13:10,921 --> 00:13:20,500
other less common types of crashes that are producing injuries and fatalities will be addressed in the future, I'm sure.

146
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:29,540
Before the advent of modern research techniques, safety innovations were spurred only by injuries and fatalities at the track.

147
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:40,880
Next, new safety requirements emerged from a pair of tragedies during stock car racing's golden age.

148
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:53,960
In 1954, Air Force Colonel John Stapp rode a rocket sled called the Sonic Wind to speeds

149
00:13:53,961 --> 00:13:56,480
over 600 miles per hour.

150
00:13:56,481 --> 00:14:01,920
These sled tests paved the way for modern race safety research.

151
00:14:01,921 --> 00:14:07,680
You're watching Modern Marvels.

152
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:10,160
Racetrack tech here on the History Channel.

153
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:14,000
No matter what kind of racing it was,

154
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:18,560
a devil-may-care attitude permeated the early history of motorsports.

155
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:21,860
Racers placed winning above all else

156
00:14:21,861 --> 00:14:24,360
and had the body count to prove it.

157
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:30,700
When the Mercedes, driven by Pierre Leves,

158
00:14:30,701 --> 00:14:34,820
crashed head-on into the sand and followed a path of death.

159
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:41,080
It was not uncommon in the 50s and the 60s to read about a race car driver dying on a weekend.

160
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:43,360
The sports pages were full of that kind of thing.

161
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:46,720
And it was accepted that racing was a dangerous sport

162
00:14:46,721 --> 00:14:49,760
and that people didn't seem to worry too much about that.

163
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:54,840
Then, in 1964, two popular stock car drivers,

164
00:14:54,841 --> 00:14:59,800
Joe Weatherly and Fireball Roberts, were killed in a pair of tragic accidents.

165
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:04,920
Both deaths were blamed on the driver's unwillingness to use certain safety gear.

166
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:11,820
For Weatherly, it was the shoulder seatbelt, which he refused to wear during a race at Riverside, California.

167
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:16,840
And right before he left the pits, they told him to put his shoulder harness on.

168
00:15:16,841 --> 00:15:20,099
He declined to do it. He just said, I'm uncomfortable with it.

169
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:21,360
I don't want to do that.

170
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:28,600
Well, he ended up on, I believe, about the lap 187 that day, and I think it was at Riverside.

171
00:15:28,601 --> 00:15:37,060
he spun and hit the wall driver's side first and his head actually snapped out

172
00:15:37,061 --> 00:15:39,760
of the car and hit the retaining wall he died instantly.

173
00:15:39,761 --> 00:15:44,440
Weatherly's death led race officials to require full seat belt harnesses and

174
00:15:44,441 --> 00:15:49,900
window nets designed to keep the driver safely inside the car. That same year

175
00:15:49,901 --> 00:15:54,700
Fireball Roberts declined to wear another piece of standard equipment the

176
00:15:54,701 --> 00:15:56,657
flame retardant racing suit.

177
00:15:56,658 --> 00:16:00,640
Throughout the early 1960s, regular cotton jumpsuits were

178
00:16:00,641 --> 00:16:07,260
dipped in borax and other solutions that didn't catch fire but could inflame the skin.

179
00:16:07,261 --> 00:16:13,060
Before the start of the 1964 Charlotte World 600, Roberts presented race officials with

180
00:16:13,061 --> 00:16:17,420
a physician's note claiming the regulation racing suits caused him to break out in a

181
00:16:17,421 --> 00:16:18,121
rash.

182
00:16:18,122 --> 00:16:24,020
So when the starting flag dropped in Charlotte, Fireball sped away wearing no fire

183
00:16:24,021 --> 00:16:24,823
protection.

184
00:16:24,824 --> 00:16:30,520
Then, on the seventh lap of the race, Fireball swerved to miss a two-car wreck

185
00:16:30,521 --> 00:16:35,960
and hit the track's inside retaining wall. Something's happened to Fireball Roberts.

186
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:40,500
It's unbelievable. Roberts is pulling low. He's in trouble. He's in trouble.

187
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:49,160
The crash ruptured the fuel tank. Within seconds, Fireball Roberts was on fire.

188
00:16:49,161 --> 00:16:57,020
the star racer was pulled from the car and rushed to the hospital he remained there for weeks while

189
00:16:57,021 --> 00:17:03,800
race fans prayed for his recovery unfortunately there was no good news to report about fireball

190
00:17:03,801 --> 00:17:12,500
that probably was one of the biggest tragedies to promote fire safety people were hoping that

191
00:17:12,501 --> 00:17:18,800
he would survive, and when he didn't, I think there was an outcry saying, we need to have

192
00:17:18,801 --> 00:17:19,880
safer cars.

193
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:25,540
After Robert's death, race officials began to enforce strict fire safety requirements.

194
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:31,040
The gas tanks were reinforced, and drivers were required to wear fire retardant suits,

195
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:33,140
regardless of their doctor's orders.

196
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:39,280
The materials at hand were aluminized kevlar and aluminized cotton at that time, and those

197
00:17:39,281 --> 00:17:45,680
were very uncomfortable, like kind of, kind of like wearing a, you know, aluminum foil, kind of like a potato.

198
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:51,791
Then in 1967, fire suit technology took a space age leap forward.

199
00:17:51,792 --> 00:17:54,160
It happened when Pete Conrad,

200
00:17:54,161 --> 00:17:59,760
an astronaut turned race driver, teamed up with a tattooed hot rodder from Southern California

201
00:17:59,761 --> 00:18:01,084
named Bill Simpson.

202
00:18:01,085 --> 00:18:06,240
And he said, hey Simpson, he goes, uh, uh, there's a new material. It's a,

203
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:09,200
It's a high temperature nylon that's being made by DuPont.

204
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,020
And he says, it might be interest to you.

205
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:12,740
And I said, why?

206
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:16,820
He goes, well, because what they're doing is they're making these parachutes to bring these capsules back

207
00:18:16,821 --> 00:18:18,820
and pieces and parts of rockets.

208
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:22,580
And when it comes back through the atmosphere, the parachute doesn't burn out.

209
00:18:23,500 --> 00:18:26,420
It was kind of a miracle fabric.

210
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:33,420
Instead of feeding the flame, the carbon molecular structure in Nomex allowed it to char and self-extinguish.

211
00:18:33,421 --> 00:18:37,900
The fabric revolutionized fire protection.

212
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:43,660
It also established Bill Simpson as one of the founding fathers of modern racing safety.

213
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:48,060
Simpson was an 8th grade dropout with no formal design training.

214
00:18:48,600 --> 00:18:53,480
But he learned how to turn his street smarts into one safety innovation after another.

215
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:59,220
I would go do a race on a weekend and I'd see something and I'd come home on Sunday night and I'd say this is what we need to do.

216
00:18:59,820 --> 00:19:01,540
And the next weekend I would have it.

217
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:03,280
And I would test it.

218
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:10,060
Simpson brought a hot rod his attitude to product testing he'd do anything to prove that his gear

219
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:19,260
was the best even if that meant setting himself on fire and i had another guy that was a competitor

220
00:19:19,261 --> 00:19:24,980
to Simpson i saw this guy come and i said hey i understand that you're telling people that my

