00:23:05,261 --> 00:23:09,920
So here you can really see the difference between Nomex and 100% cotton.

264
00:23:10,060 --> 00:23:11,340
I mean, it's very visible.

265
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:17,280
Next, top-of-the-line race tires are put under the X-ray.

266
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:26,620
Race fuels for Indy cars, dragsters, and sprint cars burn invisibly.

267
00:23:27,020 --> 00:23:30,120
So in a fire, the driver can't always see the flames,

268
00:23:30,540 --> 00:23:33,680
even as temperatures in the cockpit reach 2,000 degrees.

269
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:44,460
You're watching Modern Marvels Racetrack Tech here on the History Channel.

270
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:55,380
American racers made many of their first tracks in the sands of Daytona Beach, Florida.

271
00:23:56,380 --> 00:24:02,700
And it was in Daytona in 1965 that Goodyear conducted some of the first race tire safety tests.

272
00:24:02,701 --> 00:24:07,100
Looking to protect drivers in the event of a blowout,

273
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:10,900
engineers developed an inner liner for its stock car race tires.

274
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:14,680
The new design amounted to a tire within a tire.

275
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:18,120
Goodyear was confident the inner liner would work.

276
00:24:19,060 --> 00:24:23,620
But they needed a pair of drivers brave enough to volunteer as human guinea pigs.

277
00:24:24,120 --> 00:24:27,000
So we've got this concept of a tire within a tire, our safety shield,

278
00:24:27,001 --> 00:24:28,840
and we go to the racetrack to try this.

279
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:32,980
We pull in Lee Petty and Daryl Derringer to try this out,

280
00:24:33,460 --> 00:24:39,200
tell them to go out and run over this two-inch pipe that we've cut to blow the tire out and see what happens.

281
00:24:39,340 --> 00:24:41,420
So that's what we did on the back switch to Daytona.

282
00:24:42,180 --> 00:24:43,920
The new inner liners worked.

283
00:24:43,921 --> 00:24:47,440
They kept the tires inflated after the main tire failed.

284
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:53,580
This allowed racers to remain in control and return to the pits after a blowout on the track.

285
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,720
It's a concept that's still in use today.

286
00:24:58,840 --> 00:25:11,720
Of course, tire technology is far more complex today, to the point that each tire is designed individually according to purpose.

287
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:26,307
We do design tires specifically for wheel positions on cars, right sides different than left sides, tracks different than other tracks, Daytona different than

288
00:25:26,308 --> 00:25:28,160
Martinsville and so on.

289
00:25:28,161 --> 00:25:32,860
It's evolved to be quite a sophisticated operation anymore.

290
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:36,540
Race tires are still constructed by hand.

291
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:40,520
Each layer is carefully applied and inspected for imperfections.

292
00:25:41,520 --> 00:25:49,380
After going through the curing process, an x-ray is used to ensure that the tire's rubber and reinforcement materials maintain their integrity.

293
00:25:49,381 --> 00:25:58,760
If a tire underperforms at the track, engineers can retrace the steps of each tire's life in search of clues about what went wrong.

294
00:26:00,080 --> 00:26:07,540
Every race tire we make has a unique barcode on it that allows us to track the tire in the field.

295
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:13,900
The design engineers will use that information to look at potential improvements in the tire for the future.

296
00:26:14,660 --> 00:26:17,120
A lot is riding on a race tire.

297
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:22,340
It's responsible for translating the roar of the engine into winning speeds.

298
00:26:22,800 --> 00:26:26,800
But very little of the tire actually makes contact with the track.

299
00:26:28,100 --> 00:26:32,780
The average race fan may not know that the only thing that keeps a NASCAR car in contact with the road

300
00:26:32,781 --> 00:26:37,140
is four square contact patches, which is essentially one square foot.

301
00:26:37,720 --> 00:26:42,500
Each tire has essentially the same contact areas as a man's size 11 shoe.

302
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:46,920
This contact area is tested through computer simulations.

303
00:26:47,120 --> 00:26:51,860
which allow tire engineers to run virtual tires around a virtual track.

304
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:58,660
Constantly changing. It's a very dynamic process as the tire goes around the track.

305
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:03,460
And you want some heat on the surface of the tire, but not too much.

306
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:09,760
But you don't want to get too much heat because heat can accelerate degradation of rubber,

307
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:11,900
and then that can cause a tire failure.

308
00:27:11,901 --> 00:27:18,900
Tire failure is rare, but there is the occasional blowout and burn-up.

309
00:27:18,901 --> 00:27:25,900
Under extreme conditions, it's not the tire that fails, it's the entire wheel.

310
00:27:25,901 --> 00:27:34,900
In drag racing, the rear tires must transfer the awesome force of an 8,000 horsepower engine to the surface of the track.

311
00:27:34,901 --> 00:27:44,580
A set of crashes during the late 1980s demonstrated how the amount of torque placed on the metal wheel fittings was enough to break the rear wheels clean off.

312
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:54,720
We actually had a situation where the center of the wheel was tearing out and releasing the rim and tire combination.

313
00:27:55,160 --> 00:27:59,140
The car starts to get airborne. It takes the rear wheels off the track.

314
00:27:59,141 --> 00:28:09,133
At that point, the wheels were spinning free, the wheel broke loose, and the centrifugal force of that wheel and tire combination took it to a height that I can't tell

315
00:28:09,134 --> 00:28:13,800
you exactly, but I would imagine it's at least as high as a 10-story building.

316
00:28:14,520 --> 00:28:17,880
And that wheel-tire combination is 80 pounds.

317
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:24,180
So when it came back down, it was like dropping an 80-pound weight from a 10-story building.

318
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:25,620
It's just incredible.

319
00:28:29,140 --> 00:28:37,000
In response to these events, the National Hot Rod Association required race teams to outfit their cars with stronger wheels.

320
00:28:37,900 --> 00:28:42,880
So we developed a specification to control the strength of that wheel.

321
00:28:43,260 --> 00:28:51,320
We went into the computer, we said, okay, what we need to do now is strengthen our test requirement for that wheel.

322
00:28:51,560 --> 00:28:56,480
And by strengthening the test requirement, the manufacturers were obligated to make a stronger wheel.

323
00:28:56,481 --> 00:29:02,360
At drag strips today, cars routinely race over 300 miles per hour.

324
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:08,280
The newer, stronger rim and tire assemblies are designed to hold up under these intense conditions.

325
00:29:09,260 --> 00:29:11,580
Sometimes it's the tire that goes airborne.

326
00:29:12,260 --> 00:29:14,680
Other times, it's the entire car.

327
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:20,780
The aerodynamics of a race car create a downward push.

328
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:23,740
That is, as long as the car is traveling forward.

329
00:29:23,741 --> 00:29:29,540
But if the car gets sideways or backwards, more airflow travels under the car.

330
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:32,760
This airflow can send the vehicle skyward.

331
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:42,240
Few racers understand what it's like being airborne better than Richard Petty.

332
00:29:43,160 --> 00:29:49,100
Petty's blue and red STP car cartwheeled down the track at Daytona in 1988.

333
00:29:49,101 --> 00:29:58,800
In Richard Petty's case, get airborne, do a series of flips, and everybody was concerned about putting a car into the grandstands.

334
00:29:59,640 --> 00:30:04,600
So there was a big push by NASCAR to try to figure out a way to keep these cars on the ground.

335
00:30:05,340 --> 00:30:13,520
In 1993, a group of engineers and members of the Roush Racing Team started testing different mechanisms designed to prevent flight.

336
00:30:14,200 --> 00:30:16,040
They tried drilling holes in the roof.

337
00:30:16,041 --> 00:30:21,100
They experimented with propping up the rear deck lid in an effort to redirect the airflow.

338
00:30:22,180 --> 00:30:24,720
Nothing seemed to stop the cars from lifting off.

339
00:30:25,540 --> 00:30:32,440
Then, near the end of the 1993 race season, they tried a new device, the roof flap.

340
00:30:32,780 --> 00:30:44,920
General Motors and Roush Industries and NASCAR all went together to try to establish what the parameters were to make a car lift off.

341
00:30:44,921 --> 00:30:50,780
and they did some wind tunnel testing and finally came up with a design where the two flaps,

342
00:30:51,240 --> 00:30:57,500
when the car is turned, come up, spoil the air across the top of the car and keep the car from flying.

343
00:30:57,780 --> 00:31:00,880
The flaps are made of Kevlar and mounted at the back of the roof.

344
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:03,900
Each is attached with hinges and cables.

345
00:31:04,560 --> 00:31:09,120
When the car turns around backwards, the air running across the flaps creates a negative pressure

346
00:31:09,121 --> 00:31:14,440
and it lifts the flaps up, creates like a vacuum and sucks them up.

347
00:31:14,920 --> 00:31:19,760
Once deployed, the flaps spoil the upward airflow and keep the car on the ground.

348
00:31:21,460 --> 00:31:26,620
Stock cars were outfitted with the flaps for the first time at the 1994 Daytona 500.

349
00:31:27,820 --> 00:31:31,400
In the years that followed, the flaps helped keep cars pinned to the track.

350
00:31:31,980 --> 00:31:37,520
But in 2000, stock car racing was confronted with another deadly dilemma.

351
00:31:38,220 --> 00:31:43,660
One that forced crash scientists to dissect the awesome force of a high-speed frontal impact.

352
00:31:43,661 --> 00:31:48,780
Coming up, researchers confront a racetrack killer head on.

353
00:31:57,020 --> 00:32:02,760
One cylinder in a dragster's engine produces more horsepower than an entire stock car engine.

354
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:12,000
Watching modern marvels, racetrack tech here on the History Channel.

355
00:32:13,660 --> 00:32:16,700
In every crash, there is a moment of impact.

356
00:32:17,520 --> 00:32:20,420
This dramatic event happens faster than the eye can see.

357
00:32:21,420 --> 00:32:25,740
So researchers use high-speed cameras to examine crashes frame by frame,

358
00:32:26,360 --> 00:32:28,940
breaking the impact down to thousands of a second.

359
00:32:29,780 --> 00:32:31,780
In each microphase of the crash,

360
00:32:32,300 --> 00:32:36,420
biomechanical engineers study how the human body and the car interact.

361
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:40,580
They examine how the force of the impact travels through the car

362
00:32:40,581 --> 00:32:42,360
and is transferred to the driver.

363
00:32:42,361 --> 00:32:46,260
crash scientists measure this force in g's

364
00:32:46,261 --> 00:32:56,340
a g is a measure of acceleration it's how quickly your speed or velocity changes in a

365
00:32:56,341 --> 00:33:02,940
passenger car crash typically the acceleration level will be maybe 25 or 30 g's in a passenger

366
00:33:02,941 --> 00:33:12,200
car crash so these uh racing crashes are many times bigger than that 80 90 100 140 times the

367
00:33:12,201 --> 00:33:15,900
acceleration of gravity. So they're much more severe than other kinds of crashes.

368
00:33:16,700 --> 00:33:19,980
In race car crashes, huge amounts of force

369
00:33:19,981 --> 00:33:21,740
are placed on the body of the driver.

370
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:25,880
Well, what that really means for those that

371
00:33:25,881 --> 00:33:29,820
is that you take your body weight, and let's say you weigh

372
00:33:29,821 --> 00:33:33,780
165 pounds, and you're going to experience

373
00:33:33,781 --> 00:33:38,160
a 100G crash. That means that 16,500

374
00:33:38,161 --> 00:33:41,700
pounds are being placed on your body. That's

375
00:33:41,701 --> 00:33:44,880
eight tons you know that's that's a lot of weight

376
00:33:44,881 --> 00:33:49,380
it is a lot of weight enough in fact to cause an injury known

377
00:33:49,381 --> 00:33:54,200
as a basilar skull fracture this type of fracture occurs when the impact of a

378
00:33:54,201 --> 00:33:54,901
crash

379
00:33:54,902 --> 00:33:59,780
throws a driver's head forward with deadly force

380
00:34:01,260 --> 00:34:04,980
the basilar skull fracture is the most common way that drivers have been killed

381
00:34:04,981 --> 00:34:06,040
over the years and

382
00:34:06,041 --> 00:34:09,460
the mechanism is that their torsos are restrained

383
00:34:09,461 --> 00:34:12,620
and their head is unrestrained, and when their head swings forward,

384
00:34:13,180 --> 00:34:17,540
there's enough tension that develops in the neck that it actually breaks the base of the skull.

385
00:34:17,980 --> 00:34:22,240
And if we look at this anatomical model, we can see where the spinal cord is attached here,

386
00:34:22,740 --> 00:34:30,960
and the neck vertebrae attach around that, and then the muscles in the neck attach around the outside of that.

387
00:34:30,961 --> 00:34:38,440
And as the head swings forward, there's just enough tension in those tissues that just pulls the base of the skull apart.

388
00:34:38,441 --> 00:34:43,440
Seat belts alone cannot prevent basilar skull fractures.

389
00:34:43,441 --> 00:34:47,858
Hoping to protect drivers from basilar skull fractures, Dr.

390
00:34:47,859 --> 00:34:49,880
Bob Hubbard began tinkering

391
00:34:49,881 --> 00:34:53,980
with a full head and neck restraint system in the early 1980s.

392
00:34:53,981 --> 00:35:00,700
He settled on a bulky shoulder brace with tethers running to the helmet of the driver.

393
00:35:00,701 --> 00:35:06,160
The device then and still now is put on from behind and it sits on the shoulders while

394
00:35:06,161 --> 00:35:08,160
the driver is driving.

395
00:35:08,161 --> 00:35:13,940
Hubbard called his invention the Hans device. Hans stands for head and neck

396
00:35:13,941 --> 00:35:20,640
support and in 1989 at Wayne State University in Detroit Dr. Hubbard ran the

397
00:35:20,641 --> 00:35:28,080
device through a series of sled tests. We started doing the Hans testing before

398
00:35:28,081 --> 00:35:32,640
the automobile companies really started studying racing crashes so we didn't

399
00:35:32,641 --> 00:35:37,560
know how severe the racing crashes were going to be. The researchers outfitted a

400
00:35:37,561 --> 00:35:42,246
test dummy with a Hans prototype, a crash helmet and a racing harness.

401
00:35:42,247 --> 00:35:44,120
Then they released the sled.

402
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:55,560
So the dummy is sitting here and the device hasn't really been restrained yet.

403
00:35:56,200 --> 00:35:58,798
Now the shoulders are going back being restrained.

404
00:35:58,799 --> 00:36:01,240
You can see the the forehead of the dummy being

405
00:36:01,241 --> 00:36:07,000
loaded through the tethers of the Hans device and the head really hardly moves off the shoulders.

406
00:36:07,560 --> 00:36:09,140
It looks like a pretty good test, doesn't it, Paul?

407
00:36:09,220 --> 00:36:09,920
It's great.

408
00:36:09,921 --> 00:36:10,740
See anything weird there?

409
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:11,840
I don't know.

410
00:36:11,841 --> 00:36:12,541
Okay.

411
00:36:12,700 --> 00:36:14,100
The Hans worked.

412
00:36:14,940 --> 00:36:19,960
Without the restraint, the dummy's head would have swung forward in a deadly downward motion.

413
00:36:20,980 --> 00:36:24,520
The tests showed that the Hans could prevent basilar skull fractures.

414
00:36:25,080 --> 00:36:28,620
But the drivers had to be willing to wear the unwieldy device.

415
00:36:28,621 --> 00:36:40,360
So at that time they developed a Hans device that was fairly large and surrounded the back of the shoulders and had tethers going to the helmet.

416
00:36:41,280 --> 00:36:49,700
And there were some drivers that used it and many drivers tried it on and said that is not, you know, that's too cumbersome.

417
00:36:50,060 --> 00:36:54,300
Dr. Hubbard responded by making a smaller, sleeker Hans device.

418
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,560
Still, few racers bothered to wear one.

419
00:36:57,561 --> 00:37:05,380
Then, in 2000, crashes claimed the lives of stock car racers Adam Petty and Kenny Irwin.

420
00:37:05,381 --> 00:37:08,460
Both died from basilar skull fractures.

421
00:37:08,461 --> 00:37:15,060
These tragedies were followed the same year by the death of truck series driver Tony Roper.

422
00:37:15,061 --> 00:37:15,761
Dr.

423
00:37:15,762 --> 00:37:19,740
Hubbard wanted to demonstrate once and for all that his smaller Hans device could

424
00:37:19,741 --> 00:37:23,220
protect a driver during a 100G crash.

425
00:37:23,221 --> 00:37:27,940
So he approached Patrick Miller at MGA Research in Troy, Michigan and asked him to build an

426
00:37:27,941 --> 00:37:35,000
experimental hot rod sled capable of delivering 100 Gs.

427
00:37:35,001 --> 00:37:42,700
He needed the way to test his device at speeds well above what was capable of being done

428
00:37:42,701 --> 00:37:46,660
on existing sled facilities at that time.

429
00:37:46,661 --> 00:37:53,180
What we did is we built ourselves a very lightweight sled carriage weighing about 500 pounds

430
00:37:53,181 --> 00:37:55,620
where the regular carriage is about 2,000 pounds.

431
00:37:56,400 --> 00:38:00,260
The lighter weight sled allowed for greater speeds on the test track.

432
00:38:01,320 --> 00:38:05,260
Dr. Hubbard suited up a dummy and secured the smaller Hans device.

433
00:38:06,120 --> 00:38:10,360
Then he crossed his fingers and let the countdown roll.

434
00:38:11,220 --> 00:38:13,860
Three, two, one.

435
00:38:13,861 --> 00:38:23,340
The backward thrust of the sled simulated the force placed on a driver in a high-speed head-on collision.

436
00:38:24,180 --> 00:38:30,640
The dummy's body was thrown forward at a velocity that more than doubled the initial 40G test.

437
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:36,560
When Hubbard reviewed video from the test, he could see that the smaller Hans device worked.

438
00:38:37,160 --> 00:38:40,360
It stopped the head from hyperextending away from the torso.

439
00:38:40,361 --> 00:38:49,360
In the laboratory, high-speed images demonstrated again and again how a head and neck restraint system could dampen deadly G-loads.

440
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:57,360
And on February 18, 2001, the death of a racing legend would drive this point home.

441
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:09,360
In 1989, actor Paul Newman became one of the first race car drivers to wear an early prototype of the Hans device.

442
00:39:10,360 --> 00:39:21,320
You're watching Modern Marvel's Racetrack Tech here on the History Channel.

443
00:39:22,260 --> 00:39:25,680
Working in relative anonymity, in laboratories and garages,

444
00:39:26,340 --> 00:39:31,000
crash investigators had dramatically advanced racing safety since the late 1980s.

445
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:37,240
But in 2001, the field of crash science suddenly became front-page news.

446
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:52,140
Dale Earnhardt was a seven-time champion known to fans as the Intimidator.

447
00:39:53,580 --> 00:39:59,940
He was a living icon and one of the top contenders at the 2001 Daytona 500.

448
00:40:00,940 --> 00:40:08,400
On the final lap of the race, Earnhardt's car number three grazed the bumper of Sterling Marlin's number 40 car.

449
00:40:08,401 --> 00:40:12,820
Earnhardt turned suddenly up the track toward the outside wall.

450
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:17,420
He was then hit from the side by Kenny Schrader's number 36 car.

451
00:40:18,400 --> 00:40:22,980
What happened next would keep crash scientists talking for years to come.

452
00:40:23,920 --> 00:40:25,760
He was down on the inside of the track.

453
00:40:25,880 --> 00:40:29,760
He went up, he got hit, kind of spun him around, and he went into the wall.

454
00:40:29,920 --> 00:40:34,980
But what happened is, it didn't look like a bad crash as you viewed it,

455
00:40:34,981 --> 00:40:43,580
But the change in velocity of that car from where it was just prior to the incident to just after the incident was pretty astounding.

456
00:40:44,700 --> 00:40:49,260
Earnhardt hit the outside wall going approximately 180 miles per hour.

457
00:40:49,660 --> 00:40:53,240
The collision threw Earnhardt's head forward in a whipping motion.

458
00:40:54,800 --> 00:40:57,300
He was not wearing any form of head and neck restraint.

459
00:40:58,040 --> 00:41:03,840
And according to some experts who've studied the crash, the force of the impact caused a basilar skull fracture.

460
00:41:04,980 --> 00:41:06,520
The injury proved fatal.

461
00:41:07,800 --> 00:41:10,200
Racing lost one of its most beloved drivers.

462
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:13,040
Combined with the tragedies of 2000,

463
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:17,740
Earnhardt's death pushed the field of racing safety into the spotlight.

464
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:23,120
The crashes with Tony Roper, Adam Petty, Kenny Irwin,

465
00:41:23,121 --> 00:41:27,760
and ultimately Dale Earnhardt, I get choked up over this.

466
00:41:29,460 --> 00:41:34,680
As a safety company, we lost a bunch of good guys.

467
00:41:34,980 --> 00:41:40,520
We work hard. We work hard to keep everybody safe.

468
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:48,660
We saw through all of those incidents that safety needed to be improved.

469
00:41:49,420 --> 00:41:57,440
Every moment of the Earnhardt crash was analyzed, and in the aftermath of the tragedy, a renewed commitment to safety emerged.

470
00:41:58,240 --> 00:42:02,080
Much like the shocking death of Fireball Roberts in 1964,

471
00:42:03,140 --> 00:42:06,780
the Earnhardt crash became a watershed moment in racing history.

472
00:42:07,340 --> 00:42:10,900
Following Roberts' death, drivers were required to wear fire suits.

473
00:42:11,820 --> 00:42:16,900
When Earnhardt died, race officials mandated the use of some kind of head and neck restraint.

474
00:42:16,901 --> 00:42:23,560
You could argue that Fireball Roberts in his day was as big a name as Dale Earnhardt was in his day.

475
00:42:23,561 --> 00:42:31,240
and he was also coming toward the end of his career as a driver and and Dale Earnhardt like

476
00:42:31,241 --> 00:42:36,060
Fireball Roberts I think his legacy ultimately will be that he made the sports safer because

477
00:42:36,061 --> 00:42:41,980
they immediately started looking into the Hans device they immediately started looking into

478
00:42:41,981 --> 00:42:47,520
escape hatches for the cars they immediately started looking into the softer walls issue

479
00:42:47,521 --> 00:42:51,660
which I think they probably should have looked at a good eight or ten years earlier than they did

480
00:42:51,661 --> 00:42:59,660
But instead of being, they're being more proactive instead of reactive on these safety issues now.

481
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:04,780
And I think that that's probably ultimately going to be his legacy, his greatest legacy.

482
00:43:16,060 --> 00:43:20,100
Everywhere in motorsports, the wheels of innovation keep turning.

483
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:23,440
At Bill Simpson's company, Impact Racing,

484
00:43:23,940 --> 00:43:28,160
the godfather of safety gear is finding ways to make better helmets,

485
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:31,740
restraints, and fire suits.

486
00:43:32,440 --> 00:43:37,520
The suits are made with a new flame-retardant material called Impactanium.

487
00:43:38,340 --> 00:43:40,600
It's light years better than anything that we have today,

488
00:43:41,260 --> 00:43:44,420
and this will be in all the fire suits that are made by Impact Racing.

489
00:43:45,180 --> 00:43:48,860
I just know that if I was a race driver and this was available,

490
00:43:48,861 --> 00:43:50,600
this is the only thing that I would have.

491
00:43:53,280 --> 00:43:55,840
From the cockpit of the newest Indy race cars

492
00:43:55,841 --> 00:43:58,260
to the palm of Bill Simpson's hand,

493
00:43:58,960 --> 00:44:02,240
the technology of racing safety continues to evolve.

494
00:44:03,060 --> 00:44:05,200
And, you know, that's the history of our sport.

495
00:44:05,960 --> 00:44:09,340
It's not something that just happens once and once.

496
00:44:09,400 --> 00:44:10,220
It's the history of our sport.

497
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:11,220
People do things.

498
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:12,840
It makes them go that much faster,

499
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:14,040
puts that much more stress on them,

500
00:44:14,060 --> 00:44:16,880
then something else breaks or something else happens.

501
00:44:16,881 --> 00:44:19,460
And then you go to that and you just keep going.

502
00:44:19,540 --> 00:44:22,540
That's why cars keep going faster and faster and faster.

503
00:44:28,260 --> 00:44:31,000
The speeds are getting faster.

504
00:44:31,980 --> 00:44:34,040
The cars are getting safer.

505
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:37,520
And new safety systems, born out of destruction,

506
00:44:38,040 --> 00:44:40,720
are allowing drivers to walk away from crashes

507
00:44:40,721 --> 00:44:44,280
that only a few years ago would have been fatal

